  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't confirmed that the rule fires correctly, and I haven't opened the dashboard to check how it renders.\n\n**Fields.** The Kibana Sample Data Logs data view covers web requests. It includes `timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `response` (searchable through `response.keyword`), `extension`, `machine.os`, `geo.*`, `url`, `referer`, `host` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard.** \"EFF-b4e607f7 Web logs overview\" (id `dabf2018-dd64-4f61-9659-31ebcdc812f5`) has four panels:\n- **Total requests:** a count.\n- **Average bytes:** the average of `bytes`.\n- **Unique clients:** a unique count of `clientip`.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart on `timestamp`.\n\nI set its time range to the last 7 days.\n\n**Rule.** \"EFF-b4e607f7 5xx spike\" (id `5c23b4fa-a8b2-494d-8d9a-1d967e816e47`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every 1 minute.\n- It matches `response.keyword` starting with \"5\" and fires when the count over 5 minutes is above 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so it will only show as an alert in Kibana and won't notify anyone.\n\n**Cleanup.** My first dashboard attempt was created with the wrong title, \"EFF-9c85cfc9 Web logs overview\". I never worked out why, because the payload file on disk didn't match my script. I deleted that dashboard, and the correctly titled one above replaced it.",
